ACE Interconnect Verification Test Scenarios
What Makes a "Scenario" in ACE
In ACE parlance, a "scenario" is a combination of: transaction type, initiating master cache status, responding master(s) cache status, and response value(s). With 8 full ACE masters in an interconnect, this can yield thousands of permutations — though many are illegal (e.g., two masters cannot simultaneously hold the same data in the Unique or Dirty state), so verification is reduced to only the relevant permutations. Chipestimate

1. Transaction-Type Scenarios
These cover all ACE read/write/snoop transaction types:

ReadShared — master reads data it intends to share with others
ReadUnique — master reads data with exclusive ownership
ReadClean — master reads clean data without taking dirty responsibility
MakeUnique — master upgrades a shared line to unique without fetching data
MakeInvalid — master invalidates all cached copies
CleanUnique — master cleans and takes unique ownership
WriteBack — master writes dirty data back to memory
WriteClean — master writes data while keeping a clean copy
Evict — master silently drops a shared clean line


2. Cache State Transition Scenarios (MOESI/MESI)
Each transaction is tested against combinations of cache line states:

Invalid (I) → Shared Clean (SC): e.g., ReadShared from a master with no copy
Shared Clean (SC) → Unique Clean (UC): e.g., MakeUnique
Unique Clean (UC) → Unique Dirty (UD): e.g., a store/write
Unique Dirty (UD) → Invalid (I): e.g., WriteBack/Evict

A key example: when M4 (cache in Invalid state) sends a ReadShared, the interconnect does a speculative fetch and snoops M3 (cache in Shared Clean). M3 responds with DATA_TRANSFER, NO_PASS_DIRTY, IS_SHARED, retains its cache line state, and M4 transitions from Invalid to Shared Clean. Design Reuse

3. Single-Master Protocol Compliance Scenarios
At the atomic transaction level, the driver generates legal transactions considering cache line state. Additional constraints ensure, for example, that a WriteBack burst is not generated for a CLEAN cache line. Design Reuse
These include:

Verifying correct snoop response signals (PassDirty, IsShared, DataTransfer)
Correct handling of RACK/WACK handshakes
Proper ordering of read/write address and data channels
ID ordering and interleaving rules


4. Multi-Master / Virtual Sequence Scenarios
When defining interesting ACE scenarios, activity must be coordinated between multiple masters using a virtual sequence. For example: Master M4 sends ReadShared; the interconnect snoops M3; M3 responds; M4 updates its cache state. The interconnect monitor is the only component with a full view of all interfaces, making it essential for verifying full system coherency. Design Reuse
Key multi-master scenarios include:

Simultaneous reads to the same address from two or more masters
Read-after-write hazards across different masters
Back-to-back transactions with overlapping IDs
Race conditions between a snoop and an ongoing write


5. Barrier Transaction Scenarios
ACE supports Barrier transactions, which cause a halt in processing until a condition is met. When a master sends a barrier transaction, commands issued before the barrier must complete before the barrier takes effect. With multiple cores potentially issuing barrier transactions simultaneously, the burden on the interconnect and the verification engineer multiplies significantly. Chipestimate
Test scenarios include:

Read Barrier followed by subsequent reads
Write Barrier ordering guarantees
Barrier propagation with concurrent snoops in flight


6. Snoop Filter Scenarios
The ACE specification supports "snoop filtering," which reduces snooping activities. If the DUT doesn't support snoop filtering, the VIP's cache mirror must capture all cache state changes. If a snoop filter is present, the interconnect monitor verifies that a master holding no copy at a given address is not unnecessarily snooped. Embedded

7. DVM (Distributed Virtual Memory) Scenarios
These apply to ACE-Lite interfaces and cover:

TLB invalidation messages across interconnect
Branch predictor invalidation
Virtual address/ASID-based coherency
DVM sync/complete handshake verification


8. Coherency Checking Scenarios (System-Level)
The interconnect monitor can verify that a transaction from a master reaches all needed snooped masters in the coherency domain, that if there was a speculative fetch the data came from the right source(s), and that data was not returned before all snoop partners had responded. Design Reuse
Specific checks include:

Data integrity — data returned to requester matches what was in the cache/memory
Speculative fetch correctness — data sourced correctly when pre-fetched
No stale data — no master receives outdated data after another master has written


9. Coverage-Driven Scenarios
The ACE verification solution must define all required coverage points. Three major elements are needed: constrained random stimulus to mimic all possible scenarios; complete protocol compliance checks at each interface; and a coverage model to measure and confirm verification completeness. Design Reuse

Hierarchical Testing Strategy
An effective hierarchical testing strategy progresses from simple sequences to more complex ones. The aim is to catch as many bugs with the simpler sequences so that as verification moves to more complex sequences — where the problem space is much larger — fewer bugs remain to be found. Design Reuse
In practice this means:

Level 1: Single-master, single-transaction compliance tests
Level 2: Two-master coherency interactions (snoop, response)
Level 3: N-master concurrent stress tests with barriers and DVM
Level 4: Full constrained-random regression with coverage closure
